  /Subject (Video game research often treats games as a monolithic category, and single-game or single-platform studies leave open whether findings generalise across the medium. In this Registered Report, we used digital trace data from the Open Play dataset to test whether (H1) total playtime is meaningfully associated with mental wellbeing and (H2) this association differs by game genre. The data comprise 226,000 hours of playtime across 4,152 games from Nintendo, Xbox, and Steam for 1,608 UK and US adults, linked to genre metadata and 6,293 biweekly wellbeing assessments (SWEMWBS). Judged against a preregistered threshold for practical significance, neither within-person fluctuations nor habitual differences in total playtime were meaningfully linked to wellbeing. The preregistered joint test found within-person differences between genres under our multi-genre attribution but not under the preregistered single-genre attribution, and no between-person differences. Under an exploratory hierarchical model, the estimated spread of the within-person genre effects implied that fewer than one of the 14 genres was expected to lie more than 0.06 points per hour from the average genre effect, and a multi-membership model estimated the spread less precisely. An exploratory daily-timescale analysis gave the same picture for total playtime and the hierarchical model's genre spread. Genre differences appear to be the wrong place to look for gaming's wellbeing effects, as genre labels are too coarse a proxy for the psychological experiences that plausibly drive them. We therefore regard finer-grained characterisations of play as the more promising avenue for further study.)
  /Keywords (video games, wellbeing, genres, digital trace data, Registered Report)
  /Author <>
  /Creator (Typst 0.14.2)
  /ModDate (D:20261015070110+02'00)
  /CreationDate (D:20261015070110+02'00)
>>
endobj

